
  - [1mtasks[0m — nothing defines what a task is or where it lives
  - [1musers[0m — "users get notified" implies identity; there isn't any
  - [1m"tasks they care about"[0m — a subscription/interest relationship, which is its own concept
  - [1m"change"[0m — change detection needs a before/after, which needs persistence

  I'm not going to design a notification system on top of four unknowns. Let me pin down the substrate first.

  The fork that matters most: whether this is a single-user page where "notification" means in-page UI, or a multi-user
  system where notifications cross a network to reach someone who isn't looking at the page.

  - [1mLocal-only[0m — notifications are an in-page inbox/toast over local state. Cheap, no server, no accounts. Rules out
    ever notifying someone who's away, and "users" collapses to one person. Reversible only by rebuilding the whole
    thing.
  - [1mMulti-user w/ backend[0m — real identity, a server that detects changes and fans out. Buys email/push/away-delivery.
    Costs auth, a database, a deploy target. Expensive to add later, cheap to scope down now.
  - [1mShared data, thin client[0m — a hosted backend-as-a-service (Supabase/Firebase) does auth + realtime; you write mostly
    frontend. Middle cost, locks you to a vendor's model.

  My recommendation is to answer this before anything else, and to lean multi-user only if real other people are
  involved — otherwise the whole delivery layer is machinery for an audience of one.
